\chapter{Profiles, Mune commands, and QMR templates}
\label{ch:63}
\index{Profiles, Mune commands, and QMR templates}

A build profile selects a predefined stage or an allowed script-based operation over a planned project. The Mune runner provides the documented Lua profile commands. Optional QMR integration preprocesses project templates when the required Moonstone component is selected. Profile planning, manifest preprocessing, and compiler source processing are different stages with different inputs and failure messages.

\section{Inspect the selected plan}
Before debugging a generated artifact, run the project's plan command with the intended profile and backend options. Record the requested artifacts, source entry, tool paths and any resource generation. If a template build fails, check the generated manifest or the template provider before changing import syntax. If a Lua profile calls the build helper, distinguish its own failure from a compiler diagnostic. A profile may add a build step without adding a new source-language construct. Keep deterministic profile inputs and measure repeated builds before inferring hermeticity from a successful local run.

\section{Trace a template field to a staged input}
Imagine a root that declares \OnxCodeTerm{define entry "main.on1x"} and
registers a profile named \OnxCodeTerm{size\_debug}. The selected profile
script can return the following policy:
\begin{OnxCodeBlock}[numbers=none,language={}]
return on1xfile.profile { optimize = 1, debug = true }
\end{OnxCodeBlock}
Read this as two typed settings: optimization level one and debug information
requested. Replacing the integer with a list of compiler arguments is not an
alternative way to express the same policy. The accepted table has just the
optimization and debug fields. Selecting a different profile does not execute
this script to discover what the unused profile might have requested.

In a templated local module manifest, put the version line first and then
write \verb|source {{ data.entry }}|. Follow the bytes: QMR compiles the
template into Lua text, Mune evaluates it with the explicit data, and the
double braces produce a JSON-quoted string. The role parser consequently
receives \OnxCodeTerm{source "main.on1x"}. If the file is missing, rendering
that line was successful; resolving the resulting input was not. Triple
braces would insert raw text, losing the quoting on which this example
depends.

As a control, replace the template with its rendered two-line manifest and
select the built-in development profile. Plain declarations require neither
template compilation nor a custom profile script. If both forms select the
same entry, but the builds have different native flags, compare the two
profile settings rather than looking for different On1x syntax inside the
entry itself.

